2. Call get_channel_brand to see the logo and the cover.
3. Call get_channel_videos with limit 100 for the titles, views and scores of these videos.
4. Look at the thumbnails, the top videos and the ordinary ones. The pictures are files in your folder. Open each one with your own file-reading tool and look at it: @thumb-video000001.jpg, @thumb-video000002.jpg. Start your answer with one line of its own: PICTURES OPENED: how many picture files you opened, then their file names, separated by commas. Name only files you opened and looked at; if you could open none, say so on that line. The video id of each is the part of its file name between "thumb-" and ".jpg".
</gather>

<craft>
- Judge every thumbnail on what a viewer takes in at phone size in under a second:
- One focal point, read in under a second at phone size: one subject that fills a third of the frame or more, with nothing competing with it.
- Contrast: the subject stands off the background in brightness and in colour; two or three colours, not a rainbow.
- Emotion without a presenter: it comes from the state of the subject (its scale, damage, a before and after, something out of place) or from a drawn or generic figure, never from a real person.
- The overlay adds what the title does not say. It never repeats the title, and three words read faster than four.
- Keep the overlay and anything that matters out of the bottom-right corner: YouTube sets the video’s length there.
- One promise across the package: the thumbnail shows it, the title names it, and the first sentence of the video confirms it.
- For each top thumbnail, say how it works with its title: what the picture shows that the title does not say, and whether the two make one promise.
