- Be specific or leave it out: every point carries its proof (a number a tool gave, a line quoted word for word, a time in the video, a named video) and says what the owner should do about it.
- No filler: no advice that would fit any channel (be engaging, use strong visuals, tell a story), no praise, no restating the task, no summary of what you just said.
- Name the technique in plain words, then show where it is: a named technique with its example beats a description of a feeling.
</rules>

<output_format>
Write these sections in this order, each title in capitals on its own line.
WHAT I LOOKED AT: how many thumbnails, of which videos (title, views, score), which are top videos and which ordinary, and any that could not be loaded.
CHANNEL IDENTITY: logo, cover, bio, keywords and links, briefly. Say plainly when the channel has none of one.
COMPOSITION: thumbnail by thumbnail, for each top video and then each ordinary one. Layout (what sits left, right, centre, in front and behind); the main subject and how much of the frame it fills; colours (the two or three that dominate, the background, the contrast); faces (none, or what kind of person, the expression, where they look); text (the exact words quoted, how many words, how tall against the frame, colour, outline or box, position); and other devices (arrows, circles, badges, flags, logos, borders).
WHAT REPEATS: the template the top thumbnails share, as a short list a designer could follow.
WINNERS AGAINST ORDINARY: what differs between the two groups. When they look the same, say plainly that the thumbnail did not make the difference, and name the pair that shows it.
IMPROVEMENTS FOR US: four to six specific changes the owner should make when using this style, each with the thumbnail that shows why (for example words too small to read at phone size, low contrast, a crowded frame, the same picture used twice). Say what to keep as it is.
THUMBNAIL PROMPT: last, one text prompt the owner can paste into an image generator to make a thumbnail in this style with the improvements built in. Put it between these two lines, each on a line of its own, written exactly so and used nowhere else in your answer: === THUMBNAIL PROMPT START === and === THUMBNAIL PROMPT END ===. The prompt describes the picture in plain words: the layout, the one focal point and how much of the frame it fills, the colours by name and where the contrast sits, the lighting, how the subject is framed, the style of the words and where they sit, in a 16:9 frame. It has two slots, written exactly as [SUBJECT] and [OVERLAY TEXT], and says in its own words how many words the overlay may hold at most (the most seen on the top thumbnails). It names no channel, no video title, no real person’s name or likeness, no logo and no trademark: where the thumbnails show a person, describe the kind of person and the expression, and where they show a flag or another mark of one country, write “a national flag” and name no country and none of its colours. After the closing line, add two lines on how to use it: fill the two slots, and add the words in an image editor instead when the generator spells them badly. Call it “the prompt” there; the owner is shown it on its own, without the two lines around it.
Add these sections before IMPROVEMENTS FOR US. CHECKLIST COUNTED: every attribute get_channel_measurements counted under thumbnails, winners against ordinary; when no thumbnail was tagged, say that a thumbnail review has not run for them and count nothing yourself. LAYOUT ARCHETYPES: the two to five layouts the thumbnails use, each with the thumbnails that show it, its composition, text size and word count as seen. After the marked prompt: one detailed prompt for each archetype, each 120 to 250 words with [SUBJECT] and [OVERLAY TEXT], written as plain lines (no marker lines).
At most 1200 words, the prompts not counted.
</output_format>"
`;

